WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.040
﻿SIERRA: Hey everyone! Heads up that this is&nbsp;
the last episode of our second season. We’re&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.040 --> 00:00:09.240
taking a break to work on new episodes for&nbsp;
Season 3 and will be returning in the fall.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.780 --> 00:00:13.800
So stay tuned, stay safe,&nbsp;
and thanks for listening!

00:00:17.180 --> 00:00:24.660
In times of war, art and culture may seem&nbsp;
relatively unimportant. And this means they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.660 --> 00:00:30.960
usually get overlooked - until it’s too late.&nbsp;
But throughout history, conflict has led to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.960 --> 00:00:38.040
looting and destruction of art and heritage sites.&nbsp;
And in many cases, looted objects are used to fund&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.040 --> 00:00:44.340
ongoing bloodshed. The repercussions can continue&nbsp;
for decades after the conflict itself has ended.

00:00:44.340 --> 00:00:49.920
My name is Gabrielle Sierra, and&nbsp;
this is Why It Matters. Today, how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.920 --> 00:00:53.160
does art become a casualty, and a weapon, of war?

00:00:54.920 --> 00:01:02.360
SIERRA: So can you tell me what cultural&nbsp;
heritage is and why it's important?

00:01:02.360 --> 00:01:10.020
AL-AZM: Well, cultural heritage is essentially&nbsp;
what represents the shared common history that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.020 --> 00:01:18.300
a group of people might have. And it can be&nbsp;
something very specific, as pertaining to a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.300 --> 00:01:29.280
single group of people, a country, a region, or&nbsp;
it could be a worldwide issue. My name is Amr&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.280 --> 00:01:34.500
Al-Azm. I'm a professor of Middle East history and&nbsp;
anthropology at Shawnee State University in Ohio.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.480 --> 00:01:41.700
Cultural artifacts are part of your heritage.&nbsp;
So if we're thinking of past cultural heritage,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.700 --> 00:01:47.700
you're thinking of the remains left&nbsp;
by ancient peoples, civilizations,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.240 --> 00:01:54.060
etc. And these can be archaeological sites,&nbsp;
archaeological monuments, and also artifacts left&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.060 --> 00:02:01.140
behind, and an artifact can be something as simple&nbsp;
as a stone tool, a sickle, a mortar and pestle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.140 --> 00:02:06.980
that's used to produce food to something extremely&nbsp;
elaborate and intricate and very, very beautiful.

00:02:06.980 --> 00:02:14.220
SIERRA: So how big of a problem is the looting of&nbsp;
art and cultural heritage, of cultural artifacts.

00:02:14.220 --> 00:02:18.840
AL-AZM: Looting is a huge problem. But&nbsp;
it's also important to point out right&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.840 --> 00:02:23.280
away that it's not a new problem. It's a&nbsp;
problem as old as human history itself.

00:02:23.280 --> 00:02:28.260
DAVIS: As long as we have had tombs, we've&nbsp;
had tomb raiders. As long as there have been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.260 --> 00:02:32.040
civilizations, you know, there's been an&nbsp;
army waiting on the next hill to come in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.040 --> 00:02:37.620
and plunder them. We've seen throughout history&nbsp;
that culture has always been a weapon of war. 
 &nbsp;

00:02:38.640 --> 00:02:43.500
Hi, I'm Tess Davis, and I'm executive director&nbsp;
of the Antiquities Coalition, a not-for-profit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.500 --> 00:02:52.200
organization in Washington. It starts in places&nbsp;
like war-torn Cambodia, war-torn Cyprus, war-torn&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.200 --> 00:03:00.420
Iraq or Yemen or Libya, the list goes on and&nbsp;
on. And this is funding conflict, it is funding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.420 --> 00:03:05.940
terrorism, and it's certainly funding crime&nbsp;
around the world which is why everyone should&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.940 --> 00:03:10.500
care about it, regardless of whether they're&nbsp;
interested in culture or the arts or preservation.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.480 --> 00:03:19.200
One problem that everyone has faced who's&nbsp;
working in this area, is that so many view this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.200 --> 00:03:24.660
as a white-collar, victimless crime, and that's if&nbsp;
they view it as a crime at all. But that's not the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.660 --> 00:03:29.940
case. It might end up a white collar crime at the&nbsp;
end, but it doesn't start that way. And these are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.940 --> 00:03:34.320
cultural objects. And they really run the gamut&nbsp;
from those that are looted from archaeological&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.320 --> 00:03:41.760
sites or stolen from museums or other collections&nbsp;
in war zones and then trafficked by armed groups&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.760 --> 00:03:47.880
either to finance the hostilities, or sometimes&nbsp;
by individuals to exploit them for personal gain.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.880 --> 00:03:53.280
From the perspective of an archaeologist, of a&nbsp;
historian, you know, when an artifact is removed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.280 --> 00:03:59.880
from its context, we lose priceless history that&nbsp;
we're never going to get back again. Many of these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.880 --> 00:04:04.500
are sacred objects that were never meant to be&nbsp;
bought and sold. They are pieces that were, you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.500 --> 00:04:11.940
know, hacked off of a Hindu temple or a Buddhist&nbsp;
shrine. They're not meant to be commodities. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.940 --> 00:04:18.060
many of these sacred places, they're still sacred&nbsp;
places. And when an object is taken from them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.060 --> 00:04:22.560
it's as if it's destroyed, because you know,&nbsp;
that villager in that village they're never&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.560 --> 00:04:27.080
going to see it again. And, you know, it&nbsp;
matters a great deal to these communities.

00:04:27.080 --> 00:04:31.800
SIERRA: When an artifact is improperly&nbsp;
removed from its resting place,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.800 --> 00:04:38.540
the damage can’t be undone. The losses are&nbsp;
devastating. So what’s driving the looting?

00:04:38.540 --> 00:04:44.460
Al-AZM: You know, areas like the Middle East&nbsp;
are extremely rich in cultural heritage; every&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.460 --> 00:04:49.020
Syrian lives either on top of an archaeological&nbsp;
site, right next door to an archaeological site,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.020 --> 00:04:55.260
or within a stone's throw of an archaeological&nbsp;
site. Now you put stress, you put a conflict,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.260 --> 00:05:02.580
you put a situation where people lose their&nbsp;
livelihoods, and they will turn to looting. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.580 --> 00:05:08.520
so, much in fact of the looting that we see today&nbsp;
in conflict countries like Syria, like Libya,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.520 --> 00:05:15.360
like Yemen, is very much associated with what we&nbsp;
refer to as subsistence looting. People will turn&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.360 --> 00:05:21.360
to loot in order to survive to make ends meet to&nbsp;
try and find an additional source of income. And,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.360 --> 00:05:27.240
certainly there is a long established history&nbsp;
as well of criminal elements, mafias, gangs, or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.240 --> 00:05:32.700
corrupt officials in many of these countries, you&nbsp;
know. So, you find that added factor in, making&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.700 --> 00:05:38.880
looting lucrative but also highly destructive&nbsp;
as we see when it's completely uncontrolled.

00:05:38.880 --> 00:05:45.240
SIERRA: War leads to lost livelihoods, and&nbsp;
pushes people to steal and sell items to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.240 --> 00:05:51.480
get by. But throughout history, looting has&nbsp;
also happened on a much larger scale. Not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.480 --> 00:05:56.700
just the plundering of archaeological sites -&nbsp;
but the outright theft of art from individuals.

00:05:57.300 --> 00:06:03.420
One of the most infamous cases was perpetrated by&nbsp;
the Nazis - who plundered hundreds of thousands&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.420 --> 00:06:09.780
of paintings, sculptures, books and religious&nbsp;
objects during World War II - as much as 20&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.780 --> 00:06:14.520
percent of all the art in Europe. The&nbsp;
looting was carried out with ruthless&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.520 --> 00:06:19.260
organizational efficiency, and many of the&nbsp;
objects are still being tracked down today.

00:06:19.260 --> 00:06:25.620
In response to this theft, a group was formed&nbsp;
to save and document art during the war. You&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.620 --> 00:06:29.040
may have heard of these guys, called the&nbsp;
Monuments Men, because of a Hollywood flick.

00:06:29.040 --> 00:06:35.700
Monuments Men: “You can wipe out a generation of&nbsp;
people, you can burn their homes to the ground&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.700 --> 00:06:39.960
and somehow they’ll still come back. But if you&nbsp;
destroy their achievements and their history,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.860 --> 00:06:42.860
then it’s like they never existed.”

00:06:42.860 --> 00:06:49.620
SIERRA: The theft also led to the creation&nbsp;
of the 1954 Hague Convention protecting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.620 --> 00:06:54.060
cultural property. It established the&nbsp;
first rules safeguarding locations&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.060 --> 00:06:57.300
and items of cultural importance&nbsp;
in the event of armed conflict.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.840 --> 00:07:02.880
This same convention made it a war crime&nbsp;
to intentionally target cultural property.

00:07:02.880 --> 00:07:08.160
And yet, while these international frameworks are&nbsp;
essential - looting continues to happen every day,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.160 --> 00:07:13.440
and art and artifacts remain backburner issues&nbsp;
when the world turns its attention to conflict.

00:07:14.060 --> 00:07:20.520
DAVIS: I frequently get asked, with regards&nbsp;
to say, a place like Iraq or Yemen today,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.520 --> 00:07:27.180
that now is facing COVID on top of civil war&nbsp;
and other epidemics, disaster after disaster.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.780 --> 00:07:33.480
Is it disrespectful to even work on this issue&nbsp;
when they're dealing with so many other problems?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.480 --> 00:07:40.380
And what they've told us is that’s when it matters&nbsp;
more than ever, we want to have this culture to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.380 --> 00:07:45.740
pass on to our children. When everything else is&nbsp;
threatened most, that's when this matters most.

00:07:45.740 --> 00:07:50.160
SIERRA: For years now Tess has&nbsp;
focused her attention on Cambodia,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.160 --> 00:07:54.720
where she has worked with the Royal Government&nbsp;
to preserve and protect cultural artifacts.

00:07:55.580 --> 00:08:04.260
DAVIS: The case of Cambodia, is very important&nbsp;
because what we're seeing today in Iraq and Syria&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.260 --> 00:08:09.840
and Yemen, it happened before. And as we see it,&nbsp;
you know, a generation ago the global hotspot,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.840 --> 00:08:16.740
it wasn't Mesopotamia, it wasn’t Iraq and Syria,&nbsp;
it was Indochina, Vietnam, Laos, and Cambodia,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.740 --> 00:08:24.600
and in Cambodia, which is celebrated now for its&nbsp;
Temple of Angkor Wat, which frequently shows up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.600 --> 00:08:31.320
on the must-visit list you'll see from Travelocity&nbsp;
and places like that. In 1970, war erupted between&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.320 --> 00:08:35.940
the government and the Khmer Rouge, and that&nbsp;
led to decades of civil war, of genocide,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:35.940 --> 00:08:43.440
of foreign occupation that ended really only with&nbsp;
the 1998 death of Pol Pot. And by the time this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.440 --> 00:08:49.500
was all over, a quarter of the population’s lives&nbsp;
had been lost to starvation, to disease, and to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.500 --> 00:08:55.860
outright murder. And like we're seeing today in&nbsp;
the Middle East, in North Africa, the Civil War,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.860 --> 00:09:04.740
it triggered an organized industrial trade in&nbsp;
antiquities. And the purpose of that was to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.740 --> 00:09:10.800
bankroll further violence and the conflict, it was&nbsp;
intimately connected. And this, this trafficking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.800 --> 00:09:16.920
went hand in hand, as we're also seeing today&nbsp;
in the Middle East, in North Africa, with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.920 --> 00:09:22.380
deliberate and systematic destruction of targeted&nbsp;
groups and their heritage. You know, there's this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:22.380 --> 00:09:27.720
real connection between plunder and destruction,&nbsp;
which, you know, it blurs the scene a little bit,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:27.720 --> 00:09:31.860
but they go hand in hand, whether you're talking&nbsp;
about World War Two, whether you're talking about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:31.860 --> 00:09:36.180
the Cambodian Civil War, and whether you're&nbsp;
talking about, you know, ISIS in Iraq today.

00:09:36.180 --> 00:09:39.960
PBS News Hour: When it comes to the&nbsp;
battle against the Islamic State,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:39.960 --> 00:09:43.980
much of the world's attention is focused, of&nbsp;
course, on the murders and the mayhem it has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:43.980 --> 00:09:49.332
wrought. But there have also been a series of&nbsp;
attacks on antiquities and cultural heritage.

00:09:49.332 --> 00:09:51.060
CBS News: Several options here but&nbsp;
I think use, “This video appears&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:51.060 --> 00:10:01.020
to show … and shatter the priceless relics.

00:10:01.020 --> 00:10:05.520
CNN: More than three thousand years&nbsp;
of history, obliterated in seconds.

00:10:06.860 --> 00:10:12.660
SIERRA: What roles do terrorist&nbsp;
organizations play in this process?

00:10:12.660 --> 00:10:19.920
AL-AZM: ISIS as a good example of how this sort of&nbsp;
happens. The looting had already started in Syria&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:19.920 --> 00:10:26.580
from as early as 2012 and so when ISIS eventually&nbsp;
emerges at the end of 2013, and into 2014,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:26.580 --> 00:10:33.540
it emerges onto an already existing situation&nbsp;
as far as looting is concerned. They first and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:33.540 --> 00:10:40.500
foremost take over the trade and institutionalize&nbsp;
it. They start off by first saying to people,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:40.500 --> 00:10:46.860
okay, you can continue looting but you have&nbsp;
to pay a 20% tax on anything that you sell.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:46.860 --> 00:10:51.720
They also started to issue looting licenses&nbsp;
to individuals. So we would go to the local&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:51.720 --> 00:10:57.780
ISIS Emir who controls an area and you say, hey,&nbsp;
look, I want to loot the site, and the Emir will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:57.780 --> 00:11:03.300
give you a permit. And then you go and start&nbsp;
digging. But very soon, I think ISIS realizes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:03.300 --> 00:11:08.580
that this is a very profitable venture, and they&nbsp;
start to get increasingly involved themselves;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:08.580 --> 00:11:13.440
they start to hire their own crews, they&nbsp;
start to bring in heavy digging equipment. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:13.440 --> 00:11:18.540
they start to also host these auctions and one of&nbsp;
the biggest and most important was in Raqqa, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:18.540 --> 00:11:25.740
they set up an entire department as part of the&nbsp;
Office of Mineral Wealth and Resources. So they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:25.740 --> 00:11:31.580
institutionalized the looting of cultural heritage&nbsp;
because it was financially profitable for them.

00:11:31.580 --> 00:11:36.780
SIERRA: But ISIS didn’t only see these&nbsp;
treasures as a source of income. They&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:36.780 --> 00:11:41.280
also used archeological sites for other&nbsp;
purposes, turning them into shields during&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:41.280 --> 00:11:45.440
conflict and ultimately as a means of&nbsp;
showcasing their power to the world.

00:11:45.440 --> 00:11:50.700
AL-AZM: They saw cultural heritage as a&nbsp;
resource on many different levels. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:50.700 --> 00:11:57.360
for example, when the International Coalition&nbsp;
started to do airstrikes on ISIS, after 2015,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:57.360 --> 00:12:02.340
ISIS started to use important cultural&nbsp;
heritage sites in Syria and Iraq,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:02.340 --> 00:12:07.020
to put their ammunition dumps, they use them&nbsp;
as training camps for their, you know, goods.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:07.020 --> 00:12:13.620
I have seen pictures collected by some of&nbsp;
our activists who worked with us in Syria&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:13.620 --> 00:12:20.580
by siting oil refineries, on archaeological&nbsp;
sites. And of course, the most, you know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:20.580 --> 00:12:27.480
horrific form of exploitation of cultural&nbsp;
heritage, we see from late 2015 onwards,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:27.480 --> 00:12:35.460
and this is demonstrated by what I refer [to] as&nbsp;
a cultural atrocities. This is when ISIS started&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:35.460 --> 00:12:42.300
to go into museums and smashing the contents of&nbsp;
these museums or going into major archaeological&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:42.300 --> 00:12:49.260
sites and smashing the contents and culminating&nbsp;
in the now iconic image of the Temple of Bel in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:49.260 --> 00:12:56.100
Palmyra being blown up and all of the these are&nbsp;
pre-prepared, they're videoed, and there's usually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:56.100 --> 00:13:01.140
some sort of narrative that goes with them.&nbsp;
And they are often released to coincide with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:01.140 --> 00:13:06.480
specific moments that they decide. So they’re&nbsp;
using these actions, these cultural atrocities&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:06.480 --> 00:13:12.780
as a form of propaganda. And demonstrating&nbsp;
to its own narrow group of followers, look,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:12.780 --> 00:13:17.820
we are powerful. And so you can see that cultural&nbsp;
heritage here is being exploited on many levels.

00:13:19.340 --> 00:13:25.380
DAVIS: ISIS, whatever you may say about them they&nbsp;
were very good businessmen. You know, it went from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:25.380 --> 00:13:31.380
a ragtag group of fighters to the richest terror&nbsp;
organization in history, and how did they do that?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:31.380 --> 00:13:37.440
They did it through criminal enterprises, through&nbsp;
a mix of extortion, you know, ransom and robbery&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:37.440 --> 00:13:43.620
and looting, and unfortunately, found a willing&nbsp;
market. Something else that has changed with this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:43.620 --> 00:13:48.300
conflict in comparison with those past I mean,&nbsp;
even if you're looking at the Iraq invasion in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:48.300 --> 00:13:58.320
2003, someone on the Iraqi border can now through&nbsp;
Facebook, through WhatsApp, sell directly to a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:58.320 --> 00:14:04.920
buyer in rural Indiana and you know, FedEx a piece&nbsp;
there, and have it there in a matter of days where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:04.920 --> 00:14:11.400
it used to involve this quite complicated system&nbsp;
of dealers and middleman and transport. You know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:11.400 --> 00:14:18.360
that too, has changed completely, and so criminals&nbsp;
have been taking advantage of this technology. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:18.360 --> 00:14:22.500
I think law enforcement themselves would say&nbsp;
that they're having a hard time keeping up.

00:14:22.500 --> 00:14:31.200
AL-AZM: I mean, social media has transformed the&nbsp;
landscape here. And we see an explosion from 2013&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:31.200 --> 00:14:37.380
onwards, as Facebook becomes more popular in the&nbsp;
Middle East region, and particularly in areas like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:37.380 --> 00:14:43.980
Syria, Iraq, Libya, Yemen, all these conflict&nbsp;
zones as well, it becomes the great equalizer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:43.980 --> 00:14:49.920
because suddenly, you could be just an average&nbsp;
person with zero or no connections, you get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:49.920 --> 00:14:55.560
on Facebook, you find you just post your your&nbsp;
wares online and millions of people can see it.

00:14:55.560 --> 00:15:02.700
SIERRA: Social media gives everyone a voice, but&nbsp;
that connectivity usually has a dark side too. In&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:02.700 --> 00:15:07.860
the case of looted art, social platforms are often&nbsp;
the first step in the laundering process that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:07.860 --> 00:15:12.780
allows pieces to make their way to the mainstream&nbsp;
art market. More on this after the break.

00:15:58.260 --> 00:16:21.060
SIERRA: How does a looted object eventually&nbsp;
show up for sale on a legal art market?

00:16:21.060 --> 00:16:27.360
DAVIS: I should say one of the many difficulties&nbsp;
of fighting this illicit trade is that unlike&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:27.360 --> 00:16:33.120
drugs, unlike guns, cultural objects aren't&nbsp;
automatically illicit. You can't tell by looking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:33.120 --> 00:16:39.480
at something if it's illegal or not. It depends&nbsp;
on the history, and, in fact, for this reason, if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:39.480 --> 00:16:43.500
you look at the research, the scholarship that's&nbsp;
being done on this increasingly criminologists&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:43.500 --> 00:16:47.520
are comparing it more to laundering. That these&nbsp;
things aren't just being trafficked, they're being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:47.520 --> 00:16:53.160
laundered onto the legitimate market. The art&nbsp;
market is the largest unregulated legal market&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:53.160 --> 00:16:59.880
in the world. You can buy a car for hundreds or&nbsp;
thousands of dollars and you get a VIN number,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:59.880 --> 00:17:07.020
you get an ownership history, you get all of&nbsp;
these things, but you buy a $500 million painting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:07.020 --> 00:17:13.320
and you don't know the name of the person who's&nbsp;
selling it. In how many industries does that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:13.320 --> 00:17:20.160
happen? And I think it's been able to continue&nbsp;
like that, again, because of this mistaken concept&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:20.160 --> 00:17:25.140
that art crime is a white collar, victimless&nbsp;
crime, and certainly there's aspects of that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:25.140 --> 00:17:31.080
but again, there's very serious reasons for&nbsp;
governments to care about this. And one case&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:31.080 --> 00:17:38.640
with which I was very honored to work was an&nbsp;
antiquity from Cambodia, a masterpiece, that was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:38.640 --> 00:17:44.640
looted in the chaos leading up to the Killing&nbsp;
Fields. It was broken into pieces, smuggled&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:44.640 --> 00:17:50.880
across the border to Thailand by insurgents,&nbsp;
most likely the Khmer Rouge, and then it was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:50.880 --> 00:17:57.840
slowly laundered onto the European art market,&nbsp;
given a false history and then, you know, shows&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:57.840 --> 00:18:04.620
up for sale on the cover of a catalog for one of&nbsp;
the most prominent auction houses in the world.

00:18:04.620 --> 00:18:05.160
SIERRA: Wow.

00:18:05.160 --> 00:18:08.640
DAVIS: Yeah, missing its feet&nbsp;
which remain chopped off at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:08.640 --> 00:18:12.600
ankles behind at the site. Luckily,&nbsp;
that piece has been returned home,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:12.600 --> 00:18:17.360
but most are not. Most that disappear into&nbsp;
the black market sadly disappear forever.

00:18:17.360 --> 00:18:23.340
SIERRA: Just this summer an auction&nbsp;
house sale of two Nigerian artifacts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:23.340 --> 00:18:26.940
led to a public outcry after an&nbsp;
article claimed the sculptures&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:26.940 --> 00:18:31.440
were likely stolen from Nigeria in&nbsp;
the late 1960s during its civil war.

00:18:31.440 --> 00:18:36.780
And it isn’t just the auction houses or museums&nbsp;
that are guilty of these sales and acquisitions.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:36.780 --> 00:18:42.660
Take the crafts store Hobby Lobby, which was&nbsp;
forced to forfeit more than 5,500 ancient&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:42.660 --> 00:18:47.820
tablets and seals after it was found that they&nbsp;
were illegally smuggled artifacts from Iraq.

00:18:47.820 --> 00:18:53.580
These crimes, unfortunately, are common,&nbsp;
and often go undetected or unresolved.

00:18:53.580 --> 00:18:59.100
SIERRA: So what international laws are there&nbsp;
when it comes to cultural heritage and war?

00:18:59.100 --> 00:19:04.140
DAVIS: The legal framework for this really&nbsp;
only developed in recent decades. The main&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:04.140 --> 00:19:09.000
international law is a treaty from&nbsp;
1970, the 1970 UNESCO Convention,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:09.000 --> 00:19:13.640
but also, national law plays a very strong role.

00:19:13.640 --> 00:19:20.760
SIERRA: This 1970 convention was created by&nbsp;
the United Nations Educational, Scientific and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:20.760 --> 00:19:26.940
Cultural Organization or UNESCO. At its core,&nbsp;
the agreement sets out to prevent the illicit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:26.940 --> 00:19:32.280
trafficking of cultural property of all kinds.&nbsp;
But the designation of cultural property is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:32.280 --> 00:19:37.080
left to each individual state, so the rules can&nbsp;
be quite different depending on where you are.

00:19:37.080 --> 00:19:42.060
DAVIS: And the laws around this are very&nbsp;
complicated because they vary from country&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:42.060 --> 00:19:46.800
to country. In the U.S., they vary from&nbsp;
state to state, and because of that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:46.800 --> 00:19:51.780
it's quite a challenge for countries to&nbsp;
recover and get these pieces back. For&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:51.780 --> 00:19:57.660
every headline you see of a piece going&nbsp;
back to Egypt or to Italy or to Cambodia,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:57.660 --> 00:20:03.540
those are by far the exceptions, not the&nbsp;
rule. Even in the United States, which has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:03.540 --> 00:20:08.040
some of the more favorable laws to claimants&nbsp;
in the world, it's quite the uphill battle.

00:20:08.040 --> 00:20:12.780
SIERRA: When you were telling the story, and you&nbsp;
were like, yeah, and then the piece appeared on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:12.780 --> 00:20:17.280
the cover of, you know, a very prominent&nbsp;
art house, you know, auction house, like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:17.280 --> 00:20:22.200
isn't that the moment where you were like, the&nbsp;
jig is up, we found it. How does that work?

00:20:22.200 --> 00:20:26.460
DAVIS: You would think right? And if only&nbsp;
it were that easy, but unfortunately,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:26.460 --> 00:20:30.240
the way these trials are done, the way&nbsp;
these cases are done in the United States,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:30.240 --> 00:20:35.280
and there's a similar framework in place in&nbsp;
the United Kingdom, is they're prosecuted under&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:35.280 --> 00:20:39.960
stolen property laws. The law that's used here&nbsp;
in the U.S., the National Stolen Property Act,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:39.960 --> 00:20:44.700
it has nothing to do with cultural heritage.&nbsp;
So lawyers have been very creative in applying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:44.700 --> 00:20:49.440
these laws, but to use the example of the piece&nbsp;
from Cambodia, and this is true for for most of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:49.440 --> 00:20:55.260
these cases that are done in the U.S. and both&nbsp;
the UK, the government had to show that first,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:55.260 --> 00:21:00.240
there was a law in place in Cambodia that&nbsp;
claimed ownership of the statue. Second,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:00.240 --> 00:21:05.160
they had to show that the statue was from&nbsp;
Cambodia's current borders. And third,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:05.160 --> 00:21:12.060
you also have to show that the piece was removed&nbsp;
after the law went into place. And when a looter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:12.060 --> 00:21:18.240
is the first person to see a statue coming out of&nbsp;
the ground, it's rather hard to show from which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:18.240 --> 00:21:24.900
side it was taken and when. And you walk through&nbsp;
many museums, and you do have to ask yourself,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:24.900 --> 00:21:30.360
how did these pieces get here? In many cases&nbsp;
it's not behavior that we would condone today.

00:21:30.960 --> 00:21:36.840
SIERRA: What about the targeting of cultural&nbsp;
heritage during wartime? Do those rules,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:36.840 --> 00:21:39.000
sort of, intersect with these other issues?

00:21:39.000 --> 00:21:46.320
DAVIS: As I mentioned, you know, plunder and&nbsp;
destruction, they often go hand in hand. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:46.320 --> 00:21:51.840
the connection between cultural destruction&nbsp;
and genocide is a very clear one. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:51.840 --> 00:21:57.060
the man who was the mastermind behind the Genocide&nbsp;
Convention had actually felt very strongly that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:57.060 --> 00:22:03.060
cultural destruction be included as a definition&nbsp;
requirement of genocide. And if you think about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:03.060 --> 00:22:07.620
it, that makes sense. I mean, if you're going to&nbsp;
destroy a people, you have to destroy everything&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:07.620 --> 00:22:12.300
that made them great too. You don't want these&nbsp;
reminders of wonderful things that they did.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:12.300 --> 00:22:17.700
And likewise, if you're willing to destroy&nbsp;
everything that's sacred to a people, the next&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:17.700 --> 00:22:24.420
step is to destroy the people themselves. There's&nbsp;
a very, very dark connection there. And again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:24.420 --> 00:22:32.220
we see this with the Nazis, we see this with the&nbsp;
Khmer Rouge in Cambodia who targeted Cham mosques,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:32.220 --> 00:22:37.140
Catholic churches, and contemporary Buddhist&nbsp;
temples. And it's something we have definitely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:37.140 --> 00:22:42.260
seen particularly by Daesh, by ISIS, throughout&nbsp;
the Middle East and North Africa today.

00:22:42.260 --> 00:22:49.260
SIERRA: The history of these injustices is long&nbsp;
- but people do push back. It’s a tough job,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:49.260 --> 00:22:53.940
and it often falls to individuals and local&nbsp;
activists, many of whom risk their lives&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:53.940 --> 00:22:59.580
to protect cultural heritage. One of those&nbsp;
people is Amr, who in 2014 helped organize&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:59.580 --> 00:23:04.620
support for activists to protect mosaics&nbsp;
in the war-torn city of Ma`arat, Syria.

00:23:04.620 --> 00:23:11.460
AL-AZM: The museum in Ma`arat contains&nbsp;
the largest and most important collection&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:11.460 --> 00:23:18.600
of mosaics from the UNESCO designated world&nbsp;
heritage sites of the Dead Cities, so there's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:18.600 --> 00:23:23.760
a unique and extremely important collection of&nbsp;
mosaics stored and displayed in that Museum,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:23.760 --> 00:23:29.700
and it was totally exposed. We use very simple&nbsp;
techniques such as just literally covering the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:29.700 --> 00:23:35.100
walls of the mosaics with cloth and then&nbsp;
putting sandbags against it. This proved&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:35.100 --> 00:23:39.240
to be an extremely effective way to protect&nbsp;
the museum because a few months after that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:39.240 --> 00:23:44.880
the museum was hit by a Syrian regime airstrike.&nbsp;
It caused extensive damage to the courtyard,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:44.880 --> 00:23:50.940
but the mosaics inside the museum were protected&nbsp;
from the blast because of the sandbags we had put&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:50.940 --> 00:23:57.660
on it. This museum has since been hit two more&nbsp;
times as well. And in every time that the museum&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:57.660 --> 00:24:03.600
was hit the contents were always protected.&nbsp;
And we've done that in other museums as well.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:03.600 --> 00:24:09.660
The other thing we do, we also do, is we send&nbsp;
out site monitors. These are local volunteers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:09.660 --> 00:24:17.700
local activists again who go out and essentially&nbsp;
try to document looting activities and, you know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:17.700 --> 00:24:22.620
any damage or destruction that might occur to&nbsp;
a cultural heritage site, you know, we have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:22.620 --> 00:24:27.780
teams out in Idlib that go out on a regular&nbsp;
basis and putting their lives in danger here.

00:24:29.720 --> 00:24:33.540
SIERRA: What can a vulnerable&nbsp;
country do to make itself more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:33.540 --> 00:24:36.480
resilient to looting? And what&nbsp;
can the rest of the world do?

00:24:36.480 --> 00:24:41.160
DAVIS: Criminologists will tell you that&nbsp;
no illicit trade in history has ever been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:41.160 --> 00:24:47.400
defeated at its source. It just doesn't happen,&nbsp;
it is a problem of demand. And right now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:47.400 --> 00:24:52.260
the United States is the largest art market&nbsp;
in the world we’re the source of that demand,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:52.260 --> 00:24:58.440
both licit and illicit. So really, it takes&nbsp;
action by countries like the United States,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:58.440 --> 00:25:05.400
places like Europe, to stop that demand, these&nbsp;
sites were intact, in some cases for millennia,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:05.400 --> 00:25:11.340
until there was a market for the material in them,&nbsp;
and then they were destroyed in a matter of years.

00:25:12.060 --> 00:25:17.640
SIERRA: So spreading the word about how&nbsp;
these things are stolen and laundered, helps.

00:25:17.640 --> 00:25:23.040
DAVIS: It helps. In fact, I think it's the most&nbsp;
important thing that can be done. It is hugely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:23.040 --> 00:25:28.920
important that buyers beware. And there certainly&nbsp;
is a legitimate market. But the art market,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:28.920 --> 00:25:34.620
you know, it's a market that's very easy for&nbsp;
criminals to take advantage of, because of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:34.620 --> 00:25:41.580
lack of regulation because of this long standing&nbsp;
culture of secrecy. And so it really is on buyers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:41.580 --> 00:25:46.320
on the sellers, to do their homework to&nbsp;
do the so called due diligence and to make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:46.320 --> 00:25:51.600
sure that what they're purchasing is not the&nbsp;
product of crime or conflict or terrorism.

00:25:51.600 --> 00:25:57.360
SIERRA: It's hard listening to you because I&nbsp;
love museums and it's really unsettling to me&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:57.360 --> 00:26:01.980
to feel like objects I've looked at a museum&nbsp;
or sought out in other countries at museums,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:01.980 --> 00:26:06.780
I mean, they just have so much blood&nbsp;
and crime in their history. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:06.780 --> 00:26:11.040
how do you know which museum is the&nbsp;
right one, where it's supposed to be?

00:26:11.040 --> 00:26:17.640
DAVIS: I do think museums have come a long&nbsp;
way in recent years. When reports started&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:17.640 --> 00:26:22.260
emerging of the looting and trafficking that&nbsp;
was happening in ISIS occupied territory,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:22.260 --> 00:26:28.620
museums were sounding the alarm to help fight back&nbsp;
against that illicit trade. It's a very different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:28.620 --> 00:26:33.300
reaction than we saw during the Cambodian Civil&nbsp;
War when, you know, I've talked to curators who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:33.300 --> 00:26:37.320
were at some quite prominent museums at the&nbsp;
time who said Cambodian pieces were coming in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:37.320 --> 00:26:44.640
in the 1970s with dirt and leaves still stuck on&nbsp;
them. So, things have come a long way. That said,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:44.640 --> 00:26:50.160
we do still see museums, including those here&nbsp;
in the United States, you know, getting caught&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:50.160 --> 00:26:54.720
with pieces that were looted, in some cases&nbsp;
recently looted. One prominent Museum in New&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:54.720 --> 00:26:59.700
York recently had to return a gold sarcophagus&nbsp;
to Egypt that was looted during the Revolution.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:00.240 --> 00:27:06.000
This isn't a piece that came out decades ago.&nbsp;
So this is still very much a problem. That&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:06.000 --> 00:27:09.720
said, again, museums can really be a&nbsp;
force for good in this if they want to.

00:27:09.720 --> 00:27:15.780
SIERRA: The question inevitably becomes,&nbsp;
where do artifacts belong? Somewhere safe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:15.780 --> 00:27:21.540
from a conflict zone far away? Somewhere where&nbsp;
everyone can see it? Or do they belong at home,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:21.540 --> 00:27:23.700
to the culture and country that they come from?

00:27:24.260 --> 00:27:29.940
DAVIS: Cultural heritage is a key part of&nbsp;
identity. This is the cultural property,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:29.940 --> 00:27:36.600
to use the legal term, of these communities of&nbsp;
these countries. And those who remove it, they're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:36.600 --> 00:27:41.520
thieves, pure and simple. And unfortunately,&nbsp;
in many cases, they're also war profiteers.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:42.180 --> 00:27:47.640
And so, yes, I do think that it's up&nbsp;
to the country to decide what happens&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:47.640 --> 00:27:52.740
to its cultural heritage. That said, they're&nbsp;
understandably quite proud of their cultural&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:52.740 --> 00:27:57.840
heritage. They want people to see their&nbsp;
antiquities in museums around the world.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:57.840 --> 00:28:03.180
They just want it to be in a responsible way,&nbsp;
and there are ways to achieve that. You know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:03.180 --> 00:28:09.000
why do Western museums need to own these? The&nbsp;
focus shouldn't be on ownership. The focus should&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:09.000 --> 00:28:14.640
be on working with countries around the world&nbsp;
to secure loans, so these pieces can be seen in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:14.640 --> 00:28:18.080
a responsible way. And that is something&nbsp;
we're seeing starting to happen as well.

00:28:18.080 --> 00:28:24.000
SIERRA: Do you feel that the coverage of&nbsp;
these kinds of lootings or destruction&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:24.000 --> 00:28:31.320
isn't as obvious to people because when we&nbsp;
talk about war, we focus on lost lives. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:31.320 --> 00:28:35.640
that sometimes talking about, you know,&nbsp;
a piece at a museum could almost seem,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:35.640 --> 00:28:39.620
not frivolous, but you know, not&nbsp;
have as much weight to people?

00:28:39.620 --> 00:28:47.160
AL-AZM: You know, we do face a binary,&nbsp;
especially, where you have horrific conflicts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:47.160 --> 00:28:53.160
ongoing conflicts, and me being a Syrian,&nbsp;
I've been asked before, in the face of such&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:53.160 --> 00:28:58.380
a terrible humanitarian catastrophe, why are&nbsp;
you so worried? Why are you focused on cultural&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:58.380 --> 00:29:03.900
heritage? I reject this binary. I think you can do&nbsp;
both. And I would go a step further and say this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:04.500 --> 00:29:09.600
a conflict like Syria has to end one day. And&nbsp;
when it does, Syrians are going to have to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:09.600 --> 00:29:16.020
get together. And they're going to have to try to&nbsp;
find what common denominators exist between them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:16.560 --> 00:29:22.260
what makes a Syrian a Syrian. Syrian society, as a&nbsp;
result of this conflict, has ruptured across every&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:22.260 --> 00:29:28.860
possible cleavage, you know, social, sectarian,&nbsp;
economic, religious. What bridges are left,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:28.860 --> 00:29:34.500
that they can communicate across? And I think&nbsp;
this is where shared common history, this is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:34.500 --> 00:29:39.540
where their cultural heritage can play a vital&nbsp;
role in that. It could be a connecting point,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:39.540 --> 00:29:44.280
it could be something that brings people&nbsp;
together. And if you think of the museums&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:44.280 --> 00:29:51.300
and the cultural heritage sites, these could&nbsp;
become safe spaces, a space where Syrians,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:51.300 --> 00:29:57.180
can come together, meet in an environment&nbsp;
where it doesn't matter who you were,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:57.180 --> 00:30:02.820
what side you fought on, it connects you to&nbsp;
the other side. So, I see cultural heritage&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:02.820 --> 00:30:09.540
and the preservation of cultural heritage,&nbsp;
not just as saving Syria’s past in this case,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:09.540 --> 00:30:16.320
but it's also a key to its future too. Without&nbsp;
that past shared heritage, there is going to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:16.320 --> 00:30:22.800
be no future Syria, because it's the only&nbsp;
thing left that holds the society together.

00:30:30.260 --> 00:30:31.260
SIERRA:&nbsp;&nbsp;

